  <p>A custom landing page lets you replace a single product's page with your own design, written as HTML. It is built for you by an AI agent rather than in a page builder, so you describe the page you want and the agent writes, previews, and publishes it.</p>
  <p>This is per-product and separate from your <a href="124-your-gumroad-profile-page#custom-landing-page" target="_self">custom profile page</a>, which replaces your whole store front page.</p>
  <h3 id="What-a-custom-landing-page-replaces-Qm4tV">What a custom landing page replaces</h3>
  <p>A published landing page replaces the entire product page at your product's usual URL, including the price and the native "I want this!" button. Everything a buyer sees on that page comes from your HTML.</p>
  <p>Nothing else about the product changes. Your files, versions, price, discount codes, receipts, and checkout all keep working the way they did, and the checkout itself is still Gumroad's.</p>
  <h3 id="Build-the-page-with-an-agent-Rt8pD">Build the page with an agent</h3>
  <p>Open the product in your dashboard, click Share, and find the Landing page section. Click Copy prompt to copy a ready-made prompt for the page, or Show prompt to read it first. The prompt already contains the rules an agent needs to follow, so you can paste it as-is and then describe what you want the page to look like.</p>
  <p>You have two ways to run it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Your own AI agent, such as Claude Code, Cursor, or Codex. The prompt asks it to build the page and publish it with the <a href="https://gumroad.com/api#api-cli" target="_blank">Gumroad command line tool</a>: <code>gumroad products page preview</code> to check the page without publishing, <code>gumroad products page publish</code> to put it live, <code>gumroad products page url</code> to get the public link, and <code>gumroad products page clear</code> to remove it. You can run those commands yourself if you prefer.</li>
    <li>The Agent tab in your Gumroad navigation. It can read your product, write the page, and publish it for you. It always shows you the change and asks you to confirm before anything goes live. Account owners and team members with the admin or marketing role can use it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Only the latest version of the page is stored, and there is no version history, so keep the source file with your own work if you want to be able to go back to an earlier design.</p>
  <h3 id="Keep-the-page-buyable-Lw2kS">Keep the page buyable</h3>
  <p>Because the page replaces Gumroad's own buy button, your HTML has to give buyers a way into checkout or your product cannot be bought. The prompt in the Share tab covers this, and both the command line tool and the Agent tab warn you when a page has no way to buy, but it is worth checking yourself after publishing.</p>
  <p>Mark an element with <code>data-gumroad-action="buy"</code> to open Gumroad's checkout, on any tag:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>&lt;a data-gumroad-action="buy"&gt;Buy now&lt;/a&gt;</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>For a pay-what-you-want product where you want buyers to name their own price on the page itself, the page can instead send Gumroad a <code>gumroad:checkout</code> message with the chosen amount. That also counts as a way to buy. The prompt in the Share tab includes a working example.</p>
  <p>You can also have Gumroad fill in live product values, so the page never shows a stale price. Mark an element with <code>data-gumroad-field="name"</code>, <code>"price"</code>, <code>"description"</code>, <code>"rating"</code>, or <code>"review-count"</code> and its contents are replaced with the product's current value every time the page is served. The price is the one a first-time buyer pays, the same as your default product page shows: your default discount code is applied, and a membership is quoted at its usual billing period. The rating and review count follow the same setting as your product page, so if you have reviews turned off, or the product has no reviews yet, whatever you wrote inside those elements is left alone — write a fallback like "Be the first to review" rather than a placeholder.</p>
  <p>For products with something to choose, set the choice on the buy element and the checkout opens with it already selected: <code>data-gumroad-option</code> for a variant, version, or tier name, <code>data-gumroad-quantity</code> for a quantity, <code>data-gumroad-price</code> for a pay-what-you-want amount, and <code>data-gumroad-recurrence</code> for a membership's billing period. An invalid value falls back to the product's defaults rather than breaking the page.</p>
  <p>The full list of attributes, along with the exact request format, is in the <a href="https://gumroad.com/api#custom-html" target="_blank">developer documentation</a>.</p>
  <p>Always open the live page and click through to checkout once after publishing. That is the only way to be sure the buy flow works.</p>
  <h3 id="What-the-page-can-and-cant-do-Nx6qB">What the page can and can't do</h3>
  <p>Your HTML is cleaned up before it is saved, and the page is then served in a sandbox, so a page you publish can't read your Gumroad account or make network requests of its own.</p>
  <p>It can run inline JavaScript for animations, scroll effects, sticky headers, and modals; load scripts from the Tailwind, jsDelivr, and unpkg content delivery networks; load fonts from Google Fonts and Bunny Fonts; load images and media from Gumroad, such as your product's own cover and thumbnail; and embed videos from YouTube or Vimeo.</p>
  <p>It can't read your Gumroad session, load images or media from any other site, make requests of its own with JavaScript, submit a form to an outside address, or affect the page around it.</p>
  <p>Two different things enforce that, which matters when a page doesn't look right. Disallowed tags and attributes are stripped when the page is saved, and the report you get back from a preview or a publish lists what was stripped. Other things, like an image pointing at a host outside Gumroad, are saved as written but blocked by the browser when the page is served, so they won't appear in that report. If your page is missing something, check the report first, then load the live page and look at your browser's console.</p>
  <p>Links are the one exception. A link to one of your own Gumroad pages, such as your storefront or another of your products, works like an ordinary link and opens in the same tab, so you don't need <code>target="_blank"</code> on it. A link to any other site does need <code>target="_blank"</code>, because the sandbox won't let an outside page replace your product page. Links to a section of the same page still scroll within the page.</p>
  <p>A link that downloads a file won't work, and this is the one failure that looks like nothing at all: the browser cancels the download without an error, so the link appears dead and no message explains why. Adding <code>target="_blank"</code> does not help, because the download is still attributed to the page that started it. That applies to any file the browser would download rather than display — a PDF, a zip, an installer — no matter where it is hosted. Sell or deliver the file as product content instead, and link to the product; buyers download it from their library, and you keep the file behind whatever the product requires, whether that's a purchase, an email address, or nothing at all.</p>
  <p>A page can be up to 500,000 characters long.</p>
  <p>Pages follow the same <a href="155-things-you-cant-sell-on-gumroad">content guidelines</a> as products and posts, and we check them automatically when they're saved. If a page is blocked, the error says what to change. That check reads the text and images your visitors see, including where your links point. Because it reviews every image on the page, a page can carry at most 25 images; if you need more, split the page.</p>
  <p>While a product is unpublished, its landing page is visible only to you, your collaborators, and Gumroad staff, so you can look at it before anyone else can.</p>
  <h3 id="Update-or-remove-the-page-Jp5vH">Update or remove the page</h3>
  <p>To change a page that is already live, ask your agent for the change. It edits the published page and leaves the rest of it alone, which is safer than having it rewrite the whole page from scratch.</p>
  <p>To take the page down, open the product's Share tab and click Remove landing page. Buyers see your default product page again. Removing a page can't be undone, so save its HTML first if you might want it back.</p>
  <p>If something isn't working, or you want a change you'd rather not make with an agent, email <a href="mailto:support@gumroad.com">support@gumroad.com</a> and we'll take care of it for you.</p>
